13	 *     A rule matches a real invocation when EVERY string in `match` is a
14	 *     substring of at least one argv element (order-independent, and works
15	 *     for combined tokens like "repos/owner/repo/issues/5"). The first
16	 *     matching rule (in array order) wins -- list more specific rules
17	 *     first when multiple calls could otherwise collide.
18	 *
19	 *     `writes` is for the subcommands that put a FILE on disk rather than an
20	 *     answer on stdout -- `gh release download` is the one today. Each entry
21	 *     copies `from` (a path the fixture made) to `name` inside the directory
22	 *     named by `-D`, or inside the current directory when there is no `-D`.
23	 *     It exists because a rule can only carry text, and an asset is bytes: a
24	 *     fixture for "read this out of the release's zip" needs a real zip, and
25	 *     generating one from a string in `stdout` would test a decoder against a
26	 *     picture of itself.
